A||||5||Gold-86SE||Techies Need Help - Automatic Trans Half-shafts||||1||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||04-25-2001||04:19 PM||||<BR>I need to replace the seals, and I tried prying the axle out of the tranny, using a crow bar for leveage, but no dice.<P>And yes, the strut and knuckle have been removed, there is no suspension parts in my way.<P>Additionally, is there any bracket that is bolted to the auto trans to hold the half shaft?  The manual trans was easy to pry out, but this one is a pain.  BTW it is an eighty seven automatic duke ||||reg||1
Z||000001||TheFiero64||04-25-2001||05:10 PM||||They do come out. It takes a lot of force. I had the same thing happen to me. I used some rope and looped it around the shaft near the seal then tied another loop about 3 feet up and slipped a long bar in and tugged with all my might. It popped out.<P><B>GOOD luck</B><BR><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||3
Z||000002||thomas_l||04-25-2001||06:05 PM||||Try using some impact instead of a steady pull. Make sure whatever your bar is prying against won't break off and put a small board under the tip if you aren't sure. Hold tension on the bar and whack it good with a heavy hammer. Or get someone to hold the bar while you kick it! You just have to snap it hard enough to push the c-clip thru the side gear.||||reg||1
Z||000003||olliver||04-25-2001||09:29 PM||||I guess i must have been one of the lucky ones... my tranny's sitting on the floor and i just pulled it out with just a small pry (screwdriver type)<BR>Sorry! lol||||reg||1
Z||000004||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-25-2001||09:56 PM||||There is a tool called the "silver slapper" that will get that shaft out ASAP.<P>It is a slide hammer with a "crow's foot" on the end of it.  The tool is not cheap, but it works.  It also gets stuck cylinder heads off.  It pulls out stuck bolts, dents, you name it.<P>I thought I was wasting my money when I bought it.  Now, I think it is a godsend.<P>here's the link: <A HREF="http://www.autotoolexpress.com/hampulset.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autotoolexpress.com/hampulset.html</A> <P>If you choose to use some other method, be careful not to pull on the shaft itself.  Pulling the shaft out of the inner CV joint too far will damage it.  You must pull from the inner CV joint housing if you plan on re-using the shaft.<P>Good Luck!  Or, assured success if you choose to buy the right tool for the job.||||reg||6
Z||000005||voyagerspe||04-27-2001||12:11 AM||||you can use a dent puller, rent one at a local tool rental shop for a couple of bucks.||||reg||7
